WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:01.267 line:92%
-Hey, everybody, welcome back.

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.335 align:start size:94% position:6%
We are joined now
by one of our friends.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:05.672
You know him as the host of "All
In with Chris Hayes" on MSNBC.

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
Chris Hayes is joining us,
everybody.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.509 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-How you doing, Seth?

00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:10.710 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-I'm doing very well.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
Right before we went live
with this interview,

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was talking
with you and your wife.

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:19.886
I know that you -- Three kids,
two whose school year is over,

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:22.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're basically
home-schooling.

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:22.822 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How's that going?

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.024 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's --
Well, I should stipulate

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.826
that Kate, my wife, has been
doing the lion's share of it

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:28.028 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I do have --
She basically has

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:30.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
2 1/2 hours of the day
to herself.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
She's working all day
home-schooling,

00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:35.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then she's anchor-producin
me when I do the show.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:35.969 line:92%
So she has very long days.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:38.671 size:81% position:41%
So there's 2 1/2 hours
that I have the kids.

00:00:38.671 --> 00:00:39.939 size:81% position:41% line:92%
And, you know, it's --

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:44.110 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, if I have one more
Zoom meeting number or passwor

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.648
or app that needs some login
I'm going to lose my mind.

00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:51.151
And everyone's doing their best,
and everyone's trying,

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:52.419 size:78% position:39% line:92%
but, like, it's hard.

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:55.422 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
it's a lot of work.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:56.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's hard
to not be distracted,

00:00:56.923 --> 00:00:58.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
particularly when you've got
the show that night.

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:00.293 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yep.
-And there's a lot

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:03.163
of kind of like, "Alright,
why don't you just go read

00:01:03.163 --> 00:01:05.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
over here?"
-Yeah.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.233 size:78% position:39%
Well, I think we have
something in common,

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:11.137
which is when we realized we had
to do our shows from home,

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:13.006 size:75% position:38%
that we did not have
very good Internet.

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:14.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that true?
-Correct, yes.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had to upgrade to
the premium level of Internet.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:18.945 line:92%
It's a funny thing I've noticed.

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, we're getting
this weird window

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:23.917 align:start size:94% position:6%
into people's Internet quality
through the various Zoom,

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.919 line:92%
and there's no predicting.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.687 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Like some people,

00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:30.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
like Nancy Pelosi,
the Speaker of the House,

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:34.027
who she and her husband have a
fair amount of personal wealth

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:36.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's a publicly known thing.
-Yep.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:37.964 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-Not very good Internet.

00:01:37.964 --> 00:01:38.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughs ]
-Yeah.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:40.867 size:81% position:41%
-And then -- I mean,
it's, like, it's crazy

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:45.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
how bad American broadband
seems to be generally

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:46.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
as we're learning
throughout this thing,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:53.146 align:start size:94% position:6%
or just how inconsistent it is
across various domains.

00:01:53.146 --> 00:01:55.482
-Yes. And when you think
about Nancy Pelosi, also,

00:01:55.482 --> 00:01:59.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, the Bay Area is so -
-San Francisco, right.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:01.454 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Your mom like mine
was the teacher.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.457 align:start size:94% position:6%
Your father worked
at Department of Health.

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:06.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
They seem like very helpfu
parents to have

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:09.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
during a time like this.
-They are. I mean, we miss the

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:10.897 size:88% position:44%
because, like everyone,
you know, like everyone,

00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:15.168
they're sheltering in place in
the home I grew up in the Bronx.

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:17.737 size:78% position:39%
So we're doing a lot
of FaceTiming with --

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.206 line:92%
you know, with Nana and Papa

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.942 line:92%
And, you know, FaceTiming

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:25.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
with people,
particularly loved ones,

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.280 line:92%
is always a little weird.

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.251
But they're great. My mom ha
been offering teaching advic

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:32.986 line:92%
and sort of goals for lessons.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:34.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
My father, I think
was part of the reason

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:38.191
that I kind of locked into thi
story early was just seeing --

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.960
He worked in New York City
Department of Public Health,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.562 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is an incredible
institution,

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:45.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
has been for literally
over a century,

00:02:45.698 --> 00:02:47.000 line:92%
just, you know, an amazing place

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.036 align:start size:94% position:6%
and handled the Ebola crisis
very well.

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:53.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think I had sort of
learned from him

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:55.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
about a certain kind of
cast of mind

00:02:55.675 --> 00:02:57.243 line:92%
that public health people have

00:02:57.243 --> 00:03:01.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is they do tend to think
worst-case scenario.

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:04.417
Like, there is a kind of,
"What if this goes wrong?

00:03:04.417 --> 00:03:06.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
What if this goes sideways?"
thinking

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:07.987 line:92%
that can be hard to come t

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:10.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you're a generally happy,
optimistic person.

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:14.594
But I think it's been very
helpful for thinking about this.

00:03:14.594 --> 00:03:15.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You were one
of the first people

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:18.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
to talk how serious
this could get.

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:21.067
And I feel as though journalists
like yourself

00:03:21.067 --> 00:03:24.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
had to toe the line
between both early reporting

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:27.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
and balancing it with the idea
of being alarmist.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:30.410
Did you feel at any point that
you were screaming into the void

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.244 line:92%
in the early days of this?

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:33.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I did. I mean, I definitely
did, particularly --

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:37.450
The period where it really fel
like screaming into the void

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.885 line:92%
was when it was clear we had

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:43.156 size:84% position:42%
a very sizable, rapidly
accelerating outbreak

00:03:43.156 --> 00:03:44.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
in those first two weeks
of March,

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:47.126 line:92%
before the real steps happened

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
Once we documented
community transmission --

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:52.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
In February, I think, you know
I had that feeling, too,

00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:54.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't want to freak people
out. And I would say,

00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:55.935
"Yes," you know, "the flu
kills a lot of people.

00:03:55.935 --> 00:04:00.073
And the odds of you being hi
by this remain relatively low,

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:01.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
which was true at the time
But, like,

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:05.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you look at New York now,
like New York City,

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:08.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
that wouldn't prove to be true
in the long run.

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, yes. To me,
the real breaking points

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:13.920 align:start size:94% position:6%
are when Dr. Messonnier
at the CDC in late Februar

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
does that telephone briefing
where she says

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:18.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
people should start talkin
about their schools,

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:20.727 line:92%
about, like, teleschooling

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:22.895 line:92%
And people were like, "What?

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
Teleschool?
What are you talking about

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're not gonna
shut down schools."

00:04:26.499 --> 00:04:29.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then it was like
three weeks later,

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:30.436 line:92%
we've shut down every school

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.472 size:88% position:44%
And when she said that,
that to me was a trigger

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:36.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
of, like, "Oh. We're going
where Italy has gone.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're going where
Wuhan has gone.

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:39.445 line:92%
Like, we're all on the same --

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:42.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
We're all sailing
to the same destination."

